Adult Protective Services
San Bernardino County

Dear Adult Protective Services,

I am a sixty-two-year-old adult with disabilities who resides in a licensed board-and-care home in San Bernardino County. I am requesting your assistance because I have experienced a continuing pattern of interactions with the house manager, Vince, that I believe have become demeaning, intimidating, and emotionally distressing.

Before contacting APS, I attempted to resolve these concerns through the home’s internal grievance process. After my first grievance, conditions improved for a time, but the problems later resumed. My goal is not to assign blame but to request assistance in restoring a respectful and supportive living environment.

Initial Medication Discussion

When I first moved into the home, Vince asked me what medications I was taking and what each medication was for. He explained, “I’m the manager. I need to know. We’ve had people here not take their medication and go outside and go crazy.” I understand the importance of privacy regarding personal medical information. Although I recognize the importance of staff being aware of residents’ health needs, I found this conversation uncomfortable because of the way it was presented.

“Never Do This Again” Incident

During a conversation with another resident, Austin, Vince suddenly shouted, “Never do this again. We do not ask personal questions.” He then shouted, “Does this shock you, Alex?” I was confused because I did not believe I had asked Austin any personal questions. I experienced the interaction as a public reprimand and did not understand what rule I had violated.

Sliding Feet Incident

On another occasion, Vince accused me of sliding or slurping my feet while walking and said that I was disturbing everyone in the house. I told him I did not believe I was doing that, but he insisted that I was. He then imitated the way I walked in what I experienced as a mocking and demeaning manner. Later, when I said that I have agency, he responded, “You have what?” I fully accept that staff may appropriately correct residents when necessary. My concern was not the correction itself but the manner in which it was delivered. I believe the issue could have been addressed respectfully without imitating or humiliating me.

Television Volume Incident

While I was watching television, a karaoke segment came on, and the volume gradually became louder without my noticing. Vince entered the room and said, “That’s too damn loud.” Once I realized the volume had increased, I had no objection to lowering it. My concern was the confrontational way in which the situation was addressed.

Judge Judy During Meals

On many occasions, Vince has listened to Judge Judy as an audio podcast during meals at a volume that I found distracting and disruptive. This has been a recurring occurrence. I mention this because I experienced an inconsistency between the expectations placed on me regarding noise and the level of noise that was permitted from staff.

Bathroom Soap Incident

I discovered that the bathroom had run out of soap. I went into the kitchen, where Vince and Valentine were both present, and calmly said, “The bathroom is out of soap.” Vince immediately became angry and asserted that I had addressed Valentine instead of him. He claimed that I had said, “Valentine, the bathroom is out of soap.” I replied that I had not said that and that I had simply stated, “The bathroom is out of soap.” Vince continued speaking in a loud and angry tone and told me that I should have told him directly. The interaction ended when he said, “Fine then. I’ll take care of it.” I was troubled that what I intended to be a simple report of a routine maintenance issue became a loud confrontation.

After the incident, I asked Valentine what he had heard. He told me that I had not said his name at all.

Kitchen Staring Incident

While I was standing in the kitchen waiting for a meal, Vince asked, “Why are you staring at me?” and “What are you thinking?” I replied that I was not staring at him. He insisted that I was and then imitated my facial expression. My impression was that he was portraying me as someone straining to have a bowel movement because of severe constipation. I experienced this as mocking, humiliating, and demeaning. Later, I asked Valentine what I had looked like, and he told me that I simply appeared to be lost in thought.

I respectfully request that Adult Protective Services review these incidents and determine whether intervention is appropriate. My goal is to remain in my current residence if possible while restoring an environment in which I am treated with dignity and respect.

I have Asperger’s syndrome, and major life transitions cause me significant anxiety. Moving to another residence would be extraordinarily stressful and would disrupt the routines and community connections that are important to my well-being, including living within walking distance of a karaoke venue that has become a meaningful part of my social life. For these reasons, I hope that constructive intervention can resolve these concerns without requiring me to relocate.

Thank you for your time and consideration.

Sincerely,

Alexander Miller